issue_context.body:
When HMAC_Init_ex() returned, the secret key for HMAC was kept in the `key[HMAC_MAX_MD_CBLOCK_SIZE]` inside HMAC_CTX (aka 'hmac_ctx_st') in plaintext, which is a security hole: hackers can obtain the secret key for HMAC by dumping the memory. In fact, this key buffer in HMAC_CTX was not used any more after the secretKey was supplied to HMAC_Init_ex(). 

In OpenSSL_1.0.2, we cleaned up the key buffer ourselves:
````
  HMAC_CTX_init(&m_ctx);
  HMAC_Init_ex( &m_ctx,  (const void*)(secretKey), keyLength,  HashAlgo(),   NULL);

  // erase key buffer inside hmac ctx
  memset(m_ctx.key, 0, m_ctx.key_length);
  m_ctx.key_length = 0;
````
However, because OpenSSL_1.1.1 makes the HMAC_CTX opaque, we cannot access the key buffer in HMAC_CTX like below,

````
  m_ctx = HMAC_CTX_new();
  HMAC_Init_ex(m_ctx,  (const void*)(secretKey),  keyLength, HashAlgo(), NULL);

  // erase key buffer inside hmac ctx
  memset(m_ctx->key, 0, m_ctx->key_length);
  m_ctx->key_length = 0;
````
we got  compilation `error: member access into incomplete type 'HMAC_CTX' (aka 'hmac_ctx_st')
  memset(m_ctx->key, 0, m_ctx->key_length);`

Our experiments show that to complete 100million HMAC digests using the same key,  init HMAC_CTX with secret key only once will be much faster.

Therefore, the key buffer in 'HMAC_CTX' (aka 'hmac_ctx_st') should be cleared up before HMAC_Init_ex() returns to keep good performance and avoid leaking the secret key.

comments:
@mattcaswell below is our source code using the same key to perform multiple HMAC operations:

```
int HmacDigest::init(volatile unsigned char* secretKey, unsigned int keyLength) {
  m_ctx_initialized = false;
  // Initialise context
  HMAC_CTX_init(&m_ctx);
  HMAC_Init_ex(
    &m_ctx,
    (const void*)(secretKey),
    keyLength,
    HashAlgo(),
    NULL);

  // erase key buffer inside hmac ctx
  memset(m_ctx.key, 0, m_ctx.key_length);
  m_ctx.key_length = 0;

  m_ctx_initialized = true;
  return SUCCESS;
}

int HmacDigest::Digest(
  const unsigned char* text,
  const unsigned int textLength,
  unsigned char* outputBuffer,
  const unsigned int outputBufferSize) {

  if (!m_ctx_initialized) return ERROR;

  unsigned int hmac_result_len = 0;
  if (HMAC_Update(&m_ctx, text, textLength)) {
    if (!HMAC_Final(&m_ctx, outputBuffer, &hmac_result_len)) {
      TRACE(ERROR, "KeyId %s Digest fail: HMAC_Final not successful", PrintKeyId());
      hmac_result_len = 0;
    }
  } else {
    TRACE(ERROR, "KeyId %s Digest fail: HMAC_Update not successful", PrintKeyId());
  }

  HMAC_Init_ex(&m_ctx, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL);
  return hmac_result_len;
}
```
The test code:
```
HmacDigest hmacObject;
hmacObject.init(secretKey, keyLength)

unsigned char inputText[inputBufferSize];
unsigned char *signature = (unsigned char *) malloc(resultBufferSize);
for (long i = 0; i < 100000000; i++)
{
   int inputLength = getInputString(inputText);
  hmacObject.digest (inputText, inputLength, signature, resultBufferSize);
  
}
```

In this way, 100million hmac completed in 50seconds. When we tested calling HMAC_Init_ex with the secretKey every time, it took 104seconds on the same computer for the same data. We also did consistency tests to compare results of these two ways, they are 100% match.

I did review the source code for HMAC, the key[] in the HMAC_CTX was indeed not used (and no need to be used) after HMAC_Init_ex() with secretKey is called. Therefore, the key[] in the HMAC_CTX  should be cleared before HMAC_Init_ex() returns, not leaving it there in plaintext for hackers to view.

If you want, I can provide a PR to fix this issue. Thanks.
